---
import DocumentLayout from '../layouts/DocumentLayout.astro';
import LeadStrand from '../components/LeadStrand.astro';
import Needs from '../components/Needs.astro';
import Draft from '../components/Draft.astro';
---

<DocumentLayout
  title="A dashboard that answers one question on arrival"
  metaTitle="Field operations — data-dense B2B | Tyler Young"
  description="Dealer-network monitoring built for a technical support persona, with an AI layer that answers what needs my attention."
  descriptionDraft
  eyebrow="03 · Data-Dense B2B"
  meta={[
    ['Role', '[NEEDS: role and dates]'],
    ['Surface', 'Dealer-network monitoring · metric cards · AI attention layer'],
    ['Persona', 'Technical support, monitoring all day'],
    ['Attribution', 'Atheer engagement'],
  ]}
>
  <p class="answer">
    <Draft>
      A support person opening a dealer-network dashboard is not browsing. They are
      resuming. The design question was never how to show more signal — it was how to
      answer, in the first two seconds, what needs my attention right now.
    </Draft>
  </p>

  <div class="shot">
    <Needs block ratio="16 / 9">
      Dashboard overview — the highest-value screen, if it can be shown
    </Needs>
  </div>

  <h2>How it worked</h2>

  <h3>Density is not the problem; undifferentiated density is</h3>
  <p>
    <Draft>
      Operational users want everything on one screen. What breaks them is a screen
      where everything has the same weight. The work was ranking, not reducing.
    </Draft>
  </p>

  <h3>The AI layer ranks, it doesn't summarize</h3>
  <p>
    <Needs>
      Tyler's account of how the attention feature actually worked and what it ranked
      on. No invented mechanics.
    </Needs>
  </p>

  <div class="shot">
    <Needs block ratio="16 / 9">
      Metric cards and the "what needs my attention" surface
    </Needs>
  </div>

  <div class="aside">
    <p>
      This page is blocked on one decision: which screens can be public. If none can,
      it becomes text-only or is cut. That is Tyler's call, not a default.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>What changed</h2>
  <p>
    <Needs>3–5 real, verifiable statements — or cut this section entirely</Needs>
  </p>

  <LeadStrand
    slot="after"
    items={[
      {
        slug: 'how-i-managed-ux-complexity',
        note: 'This is the complexity budget applied to a single surface. Making UX friction measurable is what lets a team argue about density with evidence instead of taste, and catch scope bloat while it is still cheap.',
      },
      {
        slug: 'how-i-infused-design-roles-with-product-thinking',
        note: 'Designing for an operational persona means understanding the job before the screen. Product fluency in the design team is what makes that research rather than assumption.',
      },
    ]}
  />
</DocumentLayout>

<style>
  .answer {
    font-size: 24px !important;
    line-height: 1.52 !important;
    margin-bottom: 40px !important;
  }

  .shot {
    margin: 0 0 34px;
  }
</style>
